from .L import L
LESSONS={
"a-sys":L(
"A normal service either works or throws an error. An AI feature can return HTTP 200 with a confident, wrong answer. Systems thinking for AI means designing the whole product so that uncertainty is contained: you set quality targets, decide where humans check the work, plan what happens when the model is slow or down, and write down why you made each choice.",
["Write requirements in three columns: functional (what it does), non-functional (latency, cost, availability), and quality (accuracy on a named eval set).",
 "Draw the data flow and mark every place where a model output becomes an action or a customer-visible answer.",
 "Split the system into bounded contexts (for example intake, retrieval, generation, review) with clear owners.",
 "Choose sync paths for interactive chat and async workers for long-running agents; give each a timeout and a degraded mode.",
 "Record each important choice in an ADR so the next engineer knows the context and the alternatives."],
"Turning a vague goal into SLOs: 'answer HR questions well' becomes p95 latency ≤ 4 s, ≥ 90% correct on the 200-question golden set, cost ≤ $0.02 per question, and 99.5% monthly availability. At 50,000 questions a month the cost target caps spend at $1,000, which tells you which model tiers are even candidates.",
["Treating the model as the product and skipping the surrounding workflow, review and fallback design.","Setting only latency and uptime SLOs and no quality SLO, so a silent accuracy drop never pages anyone.","Splitting into microservices by framework or vendor instead of by business capability."],
"An insurer adds AI claim summaries. The architect keeps the claims system of record unchanged, adds an async summarization worker, shows summaries to adjusters with source links, and falls back to the old manual view if the worker is late. An ADR records why fully automatic decisions were ruled out.",
["Design for 'wrong but confident', not just 'down'.","Quality needs an SLO and an owner, like latency.","ADRs make decisions reviewable and reversible."],
"flowchart LR\n  REQ[Requirements + SLOs] --> FLOW[Data flow map]\n  FLOW --> BC[Bounded contexts]\n  BC --> SYNC[Sync chat path]\n  BC --> ASYNC[Async agent workers]\n  SYNC --> DEG[Degraded modes]\n  ASYNC --> HITL[Human review]\n  BC --> ADR[ADRs]",
"Diagram: requirements and SLOs feed a data-flow map, which is split into bounded contexts with sync and async paths, degraded modes, human review and ADRs.",
{"a-sys-req-s2":"Give each user journey a latency target (for example p95), a quality target tied to an eval set, and a cost ceiling per request.",
 "a-sys-req-s4":"List the actions that are irreversible or high-impact and decide up front where a human must approve or review.",
 "a-sys-decomp-s2":"Interactive requests need answers in seconds; multi-step agents may run for minutes or days. Put the long work on queues with status updates instead of holding an HTTP connection.",
 "a-sys-decomp-s5":"Decide what the user sees when the model is slow or failing: a cheaper model, a cached answer, a partial result, or a clear 'try later' message.",
 "a-sys-x1-s1":"An ADR is a short note with context, the decision, alternatives considered and consequences. Keep them in the repo next to the code.",
 "a-sys-x1-s3":"A teardown explains a system you built or studied: the problem, the architecture, the eval numbers, the cost, what failed and what you would change."}),

"a-mlsys":L(
"An ML system is a loop, not a model file. Data flows in, a model is trained or adapted, it is evaluated, served, watched in production, and what you learn flows back into data. For LLM apps the 'model' also includes prompts, tools, retrieval indexes and the agent harness, and each of those can drift or break the loop.",
["Version every artifact: data snapshots, prompts, tool schemas, model IDs, index builds and harness code.",
 "Evaluate offline on golden sets before release, with gates on quality as well as errors.",
 "Release with shadow traffic or canaries, comparing quality metrics against the current version.",
 "Monitor online signals: user feedback, escalation rate, cost, latency and sampled quality scores.",
 "Feed failures and feedback back into data and eval sets, closing the loop."],
"Canary math: send 5% of 200,000 daily requests (10,000) to a new prompt version. If the old version's escalation rate is 4% and the canary shows 5.5%, the difference of 1.5 points on 10,000 requests is about 6-7 standard errors (canary SE ≈ √(0.055×0.945/10,000) ≈ 0.0023), a clear regression worth rolling back.",
["Treating prompts and retrieval configs as code you can change without versioning or evals.","Gating deploys only on error rates when the real risk is lower answer quality.","Having no one who owns the production quality metric."],
"A retailer's product-search assistant degraded after a catalog schema change renamed fields used in retrieval. Lineage linking the index build to the schema version showed the cause within an hour, and a quality gate now blocks index builds whose retrieval recall drops.",
["Prompts, tools and indexes are model config: version them.","Gate releases on quality, not just errors.","Close the loop from production back to data."],
"flowchart LR\n  DATA[Data] --> ADAPT[Train / adapt / prompt]\n  ADAPT --> EVAL[Offline eval gate]\n  EVAL --> SERVE[Shadow / canary / serve]\n  SERVE --> MON[Monitor]\n  MON --> FB[Feedback + failures]\n  FB --> DATA",
"Diagram: a loop from data to adaptation, offline eval gate, serving with shadow or canary, monitoring and feedback back to data.",
{"a-mlsys-pipe-s2":"Offline signals come from fixed eval sets before release; online signals come from live users after release. You need both because each misses things the other catches.",
 "a-mlsys-pipe-s4":"Shadow runs the new version on real traffic without showing users; canary shows it to a small share; blue-green switches all traffic between two full environments.",
 "a-mlsys-pipe-s5":"Lineage records which data, prompt, model and index produced each output, so you can trace a bad answer to its cause.",
 "a-mlsys-debt-s2":"Small prompt edits, provider model updates or changed defaults can shift behavior. Pin versions and rerun evals when anything changes.",
 "a-mlsys-debt-s3":"The agent harness (tool definitions, retries, parsing) changes too, and a harness bug can look like a model regression."}),

"a-llm-arch":L(
"Choosing a model is like choosing a vehicle. A frontier API is a rental supercar: powerful, no maintenance, but you follow the rental rules. An open-weight model is a car you own: you control it and can tune it, but you maintain it. Small models are scooters: cheap and fast for short trips. Architects need to know these shapes, not memorize leaderboard positions.",
["List the hard constraints first: data residency, latency, budget, license and deployment location.",
 "Shortlist candidates of the right shape: frontier API, managed open model, self-hosted dense or MoE, small or on-device model.",
 "Check practical fit: context length, tool-calling reliability, multilingual tokenization cost, structured-output support.",
 "Evaluate the shortlist on your own task data and compare quality, latency and cost per correct answer.",
 "Plan for change: keep prompts and evals portable so you can switch models later."],
"Cost per correct answer: Model A costs $0.010 per request at 92% accuracy, so $0.010 / 0.92 ≈ $0.0109 per correct answer. Model B costs $0.002 at 85%, so ≈ $0.0024. B is about 4.6× cheaper per correct answer; whether the 7-point accuracy gap is acceptable depends on the cost of an error.",
["Picking a model from a public leaderboard without testing on your own data.","Ignoring license terms that restrict commercial use or require attribution.","Forgetting that tokenizer and chat-template differences make prompts non-portable."],
"A European bank needs EU-only processing. It shortlists an EU-region managed API and an open-weight model self-hosted in its data center, runs both on 500 internal questions, and picks the managed option for general use and the self-hosted one for the most sensitive documents.",
["Constraints first, benchmarks second.","Compare cost per correct answer, not price per token.","Keep evals portable so the model is a replaceable part."],
"flowchart TD\n  C[Constraints: residency, latency, budget, license] --> S[Shortlist by shape]\n  S --> F[Frontier API]\n  S --> O[Open-weight dense / MoE]\n  S --> SM[Small / on-device]\n  F --> E[Eval on your data]\n  O --> E\n  SM --> E\n  E --> P[Pick + keep portable]",
"Diagram: constraints lead to a shortlist of frontier, open-weight and small models, which are all evaluated on your data before picking one.",
{"a-llm-types-s2":"Open-weight dense models give full control and can be fine-tuned and hosted anywhere, at the cost of running the serving stack yourself.",
 "a-llm-types-s4":"Small language models (roughly 1-10B parameters) can run on a laptop, phone or single GPU, which helps privacy and latency for narrow tasks.",
 "a-llm-select-s1":"Leaderboards measure general tasks, may be contaminated, and rarely match your domain. Use them only to shortlist.",
 "a-llm-select-s2":"'Open weights' does not always mean open source: check the license for commercial-use limits, user thresholds and attribution rules.",
 "a-llm-select-s5":"Build a small, representative eval set from real tasks and run every candidate on it before deciding."}),

"a-ref":L(
"Most enterprise AI systems are built from a handful of reusable shapes, like rooms in a house plan. There is the simple API call, the search-and-answer system (RAG), the overnight batch job, the background agent worker and the human review queue. A reference architecture shows how these pieces connect so each team doesn't reinvent them.",
["Pick the pattern that matches the job: request/response, RAG with citations, batch enrichment, async agent worker or review queue.",
 "Layer it: edge (auth, rate limits), API, orchestration, model gateway, data and tools.",
 "Separate the control plane (prompts, policies, eval configs, routing rules) from the data plane (tokens, embeddings, tool I/O).",
 "Add shared services once: identity, billing, observability, secrets.",
 "Plan multi-region and multi-tenant behavior, including cache affinity and per-tenant keys."],
"Batch vs realtime: enriching 2 million product descriptions overnight with a batch API that is priced at 50% of the realtime rate (as OpenAI's and Anthropic's batch APIs are) halves the bill. At 500 tokens each and $2 per 1M tokens realtime, that is 1B tokens → $2,000 realtime vs $1,000 batch.",
["Drawing a microservice for every box before the system has one working path.","Mixing control-plane changes (prompt edits) into data-plane deploys without review.","Ignoring that prefix caches are per replica, so random load balancing lowers cache hits."],
"A telecom company standardizes on three blueprints (RAG assistant, batch enrichment, agent worker with review queue). New teams start from a template that already includes the gateway, tracing and evals, cutting time to first pilot from months to weeks.",
["A few patterns cover most systems.","Keep control plane and data plane separate.","Templates spread good defaults faster than documents do."],
"flowchart TD\n  EDGE[Edge: auth, rate limit] --> API[API]\n  API --> ORCH[Orchestration / agents]\n  ORCH --> GW[Model gateway]\n  ORCH --> DATA[Data + retrieval]\n  ORCH --> TOOLS[Tools]\n  CP[Control plane: prompts, policies, evals] -.-> ORCH\n  CP -.-> GW",
"Diagram: layered reference architecture from edge to API to orchestration, which calls a model gateway, data and tools; a control plane configures orchestration and the gateway.",
{"a-ref-pat-s1":"A facade gives every team one internal API for model calls, hiding provider differences and adding logging and limits.",
 "a-ref-pat-s2":"RAG retrieves relevant passages and asks the model to answer only from them with citations, so users can check the source.",
 "a-ref-pat-s5":"Low-confidence or high-risk outputs go to a queue where people approve, edit or reject them, and their decisions become training and eval data.",
 "a-ref-layers-s2":"The control plane changes slowly and needs review (prompts, policies); the data plane handles every request and needs speed and scale.",
 "a-ref-layers-s3":"For failover, replicate indexes and configs to the second region and test that traffic can move with acceptable quality."}),

"a-bb":L(
"Build vs buy vs host is the classic make-or-buy decision with a twist: model capability changes every few months. Buying an API gets you the best models now with no hardware. Using a cloud platform adds enterprise controls. Hosting open weights gives control and possibly lower unit cost, but you take on GPUs, on-call and upgrades. Many teams end up with a hybrid.",
["Score the dimensions: capability needed, latency and region, data control, unit economics at expected volume, and lock-in.",
 "Price each option at low, expected and high volume, including people and reliability work for self-hosting.",
 "Prototype on an API first to learn the task and build evals.",
 "Move specific high-volume or sensitive workloads to self-hosting only when the numbers and controls justify it.",
 "Put a gateway in front so you can route between options and exit a vendor."],
"Self-host crossover: an H100 at about $3 per hour costs ≈ $2,190 per month (730 h). If one GPU serves a model at a sustained 1,500 output tokens per second at 50% average utilization, that's ≈ 1.97B tokens a month, or ≈ $1.11 per 1M tokens before engineering time. Against an API price of $0.60 per 1M, the API wins; against $5 per 1M, self-hosting may win if volume is steady.",
["Self-hosting for 'savings' at low volume, where idle GPUs and on-call cost more than the API.","Comparing list prices without counting engineering time, monitoring and upgrades.","Signing up for a single vendor with no gateway or exit plan."],
"A software company uses a frontier API for its coding assistant but self-hosts a fine-tuned small model for a high-volume ticket classifier, where steady traffic keeps GPUs busy and the per-token cost is lower.",
["Start on APIs, learn, then optimize.","Self-hosting pays only at high, steady utilization.","A gateway keeps the decision reversible."],
"flowchart LR\n  N[Need] --> D{Volume, control, latency}\n  D -->|low volume, best quality| API[Frontier API]\n  D -->|enterprise controls| CM[Cloud managed]\n  D -->|high steady volume or sovereignty| SH[Self-host open weights]\n  API --> GW[Gateway]\n  CM --> GW\n  SH --> GW",
"Diagram: a need is routed by volume, control and latency to a frontier API, cloud-managed platform or self-hosted open weights, all behind one gateway.",
{"a-bb-dim-s1":"Some tasks need the strongest model available; others are solved well by a small model. Measure, don't assume.",
 "a-bb-dim-s4":"Compare cost per task at realistic volume, including tokens, GPUs, storage, people and support.",
 "a-bb-opt-s2":"Managed platforms such as Amazon Bedrock, Azure AI Foundry and Google Vertex AI offer many models with cloud identity, networking and compliance controls.",
 "a-bb-opt-s4":"Hybrid routing sends easy requests to a cheap model and escalates hard ones, often cutting cost a lot with little quality loss.",
 "a-bb-opt-s5":"A gateway such as LiteLLM gives one API across providers, plus keys, budgets and fallbacks, at the cost of one more service to run."}),

"a-data":L(
"AI projects usually fail on data, not models. The model can only answer from what it can reach, and only safely if you know who may see what. An enterprise data platform for AI is the plumbing that makes data findable, fresh, permission-aware and legally usable, from the warehouse to the vector index.",
["Inventory sources and owners, and record usage rights (inference only, training allowed, restricted).",
 "Land data in a governed platform (lakehouse or warehouse) with a catalog, lineage and quality checks.",
 "Build retrieval slices: chunked text with metadata, permissions and freshness timestamps, in vector and keyword indexes.",
 "Scan for PII, PHI and secrets before indexing; mask or exclude as policy requires.",
 "Define data contracts and freshness SLAs for each slice, and wire deletes through to indexes."],
"Freshness SLA: a policy assistant must reflect policy changes within 24 hours. A nightly re-index at 02:00 meets that only if the job never fails; adding change-data-capture to re-embed edited documents within 15 minutes makes the SLA robust, with the nightly job as a backstop.",
["Piloting on a one-off CSV export that can't be refreshed in production.","Embedding documents that contain secrets or personal data, which then persist in the vector store.","Ignoring document permissions, so the assistant shows users content they shouldn't see."],
"A pharmaceutical company indexes standard operating procedures with metadata for site, version and effective date. Retrieval filters on the user's site and only the current version, and superseded documents are removed from the index within an hour of a new version being approved.",
["Rights, permissions and freshness are design inputs.","Metadata is what makes retrieval filterable and auditable.","Deletion must reach every copy, including embeddings."],
"flowchart LR\n  SRC[Sources + owners] --> LAKE[Lakehouse / warehouse]\n  LAKE --> CAT[Catalog, lineage, quality]\n  LAKE --> SCAN[PII / secrets scan]\n  SCAN --> CHUNK[Chunk + metadata + ACLs]\n  CHUNK --> IDX[(Vector + keyword index)]\n  IDX --> APP[AI apps]",
"Diagram: sources land in a lakehouse with catalog and quality checks, are scanned for PII, chunked with metadata and permissions, indexed, and used by AI apps.",
{"a-data-plat-s2":"Vector search finds meaning, keyword search finds exact terms like part numbers. Most production systems use both.",
 "a-data-plat-s3":"A catalog tells you what data exists and who owns it; lineage shows where it came from; quality monitors catch broken feeds.",
 "a-data-gov-s1":"Contracts and licenses may allow using data to answer questions but not to train models. Record this per source.",
 "a-data-gov-s4":"Agree on chunk size, overlap and required metadata (source, owner, date, permissions) so every team's index is compatible.",
 "a-data-gov-s5":"Retention rules and deletion requests must cover raw data, chunks, embeddings, caches and logs."}),

"a-gateway":L(
"A model gateway is the front desk for every model call in a company. Instead of each app holding its own API keys and retry logic, all calls go through one service that checks who you are, enforces budgets, picks a model, retries or falls back on errors, caches repeat answers and records a trace. It is where cost, policy and portability meet.",
["Apps call one internal API; the gateway authenticates the app and the end user.",
 "The gateway applies policy: budgets, rate limits, allowed models, PII redaction.",
 "It routes the request to a provider or model by rules or a classifier, with fallbacks on errors or timeouts.",
 "A semantic cache can return a stored answer for a near-duplicate prompt, keyed per tenant.",
 "Every call emits a trace with tokens, cost, latency and model version."],
"Semantic cache savings: an FAQ bot gets 100,000 questions a month; 40% are near-duplicates of earlier ones above a similarity threshold of 0.95. At $0.004 per uncached call, cost drops from $400 to $240 (60,000 × $0.004), minus a small embedding cost of about 100,000 × 50 tokens = 5M tokens, around $0.10 at $0.02 per 1M tokens.",
["Caching on exact prompt strings only, missing near-duplicates.","Sharing a cache across tenants, which can leak one customer's answer to another.","Letting the gateway rewrite prompts silently, which breaks eval baselines."],
"A consulting firm routes all internal AI usage through a gateway with per-department budgets. When one team's script loops and burns $3,000 in an hour, the budget cap stops it and an alert goes to the owner.",
["One front door for keys, budgets, routing and traces.","Cache per tenant with TTLs and bypass rules.","Fallbacks and circuit breakers protect both wallet and uptime."],
"flowchart LR\n  APP[Apps] --> GW[Gateway: auth, budgets, policy]\n  GW --> C{Semantic cache hit?}\n  C -->|yes| APP\n  C -->|no| R[Router]\n  R --> P1[Provider A]\n  R --> P2[Provider B]\n  R --> P3[Self-hosted]\n  GW --> TR[(Traces + cost)]",
"Diagram: apps call a gateway that checks auth and budgets, then a semantic cache, then routes to provider A, provider B or a self-hosted model, logging traces and cost.",
{"a-gw-feat-s3":"A fallback tries another model after an error. A hedge sends the same request to two backends and uses the first good answer, trading cost for lower tail latency.",
 "a-gw-feat-s4":"Policy hooks run before and after the call: block disallowed content, redact PII, enforce allowed models per app.",
 "a-gw-cache-s1":"Embed the prompt and look for a stored prompt above a similarity threshold. Set the threshold with an eval, because too low returns wrong answers.",
 "a-gw-cache-s3":"Include the tenant (and often the user's permissions) in the cache key so answers never cross boundaries.",
 "a-gw-cache-s5":"Skip the cache for personalized, time-sensitive or tool-using requests."},widget="routing"),

"a-fde":L(
"A forward deployed engineer is part consultant, part software engineer. They sit with the customer, learn how the work really gets done, pick one valuable problem, and build a working system inside the customer's environment, not a slide deck. Success is measured by adoption and business results after they leave.",
["Discover: shadow the people doing the workflow, measure volume, time and error rates, and find the data and systems involved.",
 "Scope: score candidate use cases on value, data readiness and risk, and agree on one success metric and baseline.",
 "Prototype in days on real-shaped data, with a small eval set built from customer examples.",
 "Harden in weeks: integrate with identity and systems of record, pass security review, add monitoring and human review.",
 "Hand over: runbooks, training and ownership transfer; feed field learnings back to the product team."],
"Sizing a use case: 12 analysts each spend 6 hours a week preparing vendor-risk summaries, 72 hours a week in total. If an agent drafts summaries that cut that to 2 hours each, the saving is 48 hours a week; at a loaded cost of $90 per hour that's ≈ $4,320 a week, ≈ $225,000 a year, against an estimated run cost of $15,000 a year.",
["Starting to build before agreeing on a measurable baseline and success metric.","Prototyping on clean sample data that looks nothing like production.","Leaving without a runbook or an owner, so the system decays after handover."],
"An FDE at a logistics company maps the claims-dispute workflow, finds that 60% of time goes into gathering documents from three systems, and builds an agent that assembles a dispute packet for human review. Adoption is tracked weekly, and the customer's team owns it after eight weeks.",
["Live in the workflow before you write code.","One metric, one baseline, one narrow use case first.","Handover and adoption are part of the job."],
"flowchart LR\n  D[Discover: shadow + measure] --> S[Scope: score use cases]\n  S --> P[Prototype in days]\n  P --> H[Harden in weeks]\n  H --> HO[Handover + adoption]\n  HO --> FB[Field feedback to product]\n  FB -.-> D",
"Diagram: the forward deployed engineering loop from discovery to scoping, prototype, hardening, handover and field feedback back to discovery.",
{"a-fde-t1-s1":"Watch real people do the task and write down every step, system and hand-off. The real workflow usually differs from the documented one.",
 "a-fde-t1-s3":"Score use cases on business value, data availability and risk, then pick one that scores well on all three.",
 "a-fde-t2-s2":"Turn real customer examples, including tricky ones, into an eval set so 'is it good?' has a measurable answer.",
 "a-fde-t2-s3":"Production means single sign-on, the customer's permissions and their systems of record, not a separate login.",
 "a-fde-t3-s1":"Write down how to run, monitor and fix the system and who owns it, then rehearse with the customer team.",
 "a-fde-t3-s2":"Track active users, tasks completed and the business metric, not just whether the system is up.",
 "a-fde-x1-s1":"Run a short working session with the people who do the work: map the workflow, list systems and pain points, and agree on the metric.",
 "a-fde-x1-s2":"Keep exec readouts to one page: problem, result versus baseline, risks, cost and the decision you need."}),

"a-secarch":L(
"Threat modeling is asking 'what could go wrong, and how bad would it be?' before you build. For AI systems the new twist is that the model reads untrusted text and can call tools, so an attacker who controls a document might control an action. A security architect maps assets, attackers and paths, then puts controls on the paths that matter most.",
["List assets: data, model weights, prompts, tools, credentials and logs.",
 "List entry points for untrusted content: users, retrieved documents, web pages, emails, tool outputs, MCP servers.",
 "Trace attack paths from each entry point to each sensitive asset or action (for example injection → email tool → data exfiltration).",
 "Apply controls on the path: isolation, least privilege, allowlists, approval gates, vaulted secrets.",
 "Log, monitor and red-team on a schedule, and update the model when the system changes."],
"Risk scoring: for each path, rate likelihood and impact 1-5 and multiply. 'Injection via shared drive document → email tool sends data outside' might be likelihood 4 × impact 5 = 20, top priority. 'Model weights stolen from internal registry' might be 2 × 4 = 8. Fixing the email path first (recipient allowlist plus approval) cuts its likelihood to 1, score 5.",
["Adding an output content filter while tools can still move money or data.","Using one shared service account for all agent tools.","Threat modeling once at design time and never again after new tools are added."],
"A bank's architect requires every agent tool to declare its data scope and side effects. Tools that send data externally need an allowlist and human approval; all tool calls are logged to an immutable store reviewed by the security team.",
["Untrusted content + tools = attack path.","Control the capability, not just the prompt.","Re-run the threat model whenever tools change."],
"flowchart LR\n  IN[Untrusted inputs] --> M[Model / agent]\n  M --> T1[Read tools]\n  M --> T2[Write / send tools]\n  T2 --> GATE{Allowlist + approval}\n  GATE --> EXT[External systems]\n  SEC[(Vaulted secrets)] -.scoped.-> T1\n  SEC -.scoped.-> T2\n  M --> AUD[(Immutable audit)]",
"Diagram: untrusted inputs reach the agent, which uses read tools and write tools; write tools pass an allowlist and approval gate before external systems, with scoped secrets and an immutable audit log.",
{"a-sec-tm-s2":"Instructions can arrive through any content the model reads: a retrieved page, an email, a file name, an image or a tool result.",
 "a-sec-tm-s3":"A confused deputy is a component with legitimate power that is tricked into using it for an attacker, which is exactly what a hijacked agent is.",
 "a-sec-ctrl-s2":"Only allow tools, domains and recipients you have listed, and require approval for high-impact calls.",
 "a-sec-ctrl-s3":"Keep keys in a vault and give the agent short-lived, scoped tokens instead of raw secrets in its prompt or environment.",
 "a-sec-ctrl-s5":"Schedule red-team exercises, for example each quarter and after big changes, and turn findings into regression tests."}),

"a-gov":L(
"Governance is how an organization proves its AI is used responsibly and legally. Regulations and standards such as the EU AI Act, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and ISO/IEC 42001 say what must be true; the architect turns that into concrete controls, records and evidence that an auditor can check. Big deals are often won or lost here.",
["Classify each AI system by risk (for example under EU AI Act categories) and by sector rules.",
 "Map required obligations to technical controls: logging, human oversight, data governance, accuracy testing, transparency notices.",
 "Produce documentation: model or system cards, impact assessments (DPIA or AI impact assessment), and eval reports.",
 "Assign roles: system owner, human overseers, incident responders.",
 "Keep evidence continuously (logs, eval runs, approvals) so audits don't become a scramble."],
"Turning a requirement into controls: the EU AI Act requires high-risk systems to support human oversight and automatic logging of events. A loan pre-screening assistant implements this as (1) every recommendation shown to an officer who must approve or override, (2) a log of inputs, model version, output and the officer's decision kept for the retention period, and (3) a monthly report of override rates.",
["Claiming compliance because the model vendor is compliant; obligations also apply to the deployer.","Treating documentation as a one-time task instead of keeping it current with each change.","Having no incident response plan for harmful AI outputs."],
"A health insurer builds an 'evidence pack' per AI system: risk classification, model card, eval results by population group, human oversight procedure and incident log. Procurement reviews that used to take months now take weeks.",
["Translate laws into controls and evidence.","Deployers have obligations too.","Collect evidence continuously."],
"flowchart LR\n  REG[Regulations + standards] --> CLS[Risk classification]\n  CLS --> CTRL[Technical controls]\n  CTRL --> DOC[Cards, assessments, eval reports]\n  CTRL --> LOGS[(Logs + approvals)]\n  DOC --> EV[Evidence pack]\n  LOGS --> EV\n  EV --> AUD[Auditors / customers]",
"Diagram: regulations lead to risk classification and technical controls, which produce documentation and logs that form an evidence pack for auditors and customers.",
{"a-gov-fw-s1":"The EU AI Act sorts uses into prohibited, high-risk, limited-risk (transparency duties) and minimal-risk, with separate rules for general-purpose AI models.",
 "a-gov-fw-s2":"NIST AI RMF organizes risk work into four functions: Govern, Map, Measure and Manage. It is voluntary but widely used.",
 "a-gov-fw-s3":"ISO/IEC 42001 is a certifiable management-system standard for AI, similar in style to ISO 27001 for security.",
 "a-gov-ops-s2":"An impact assessment describes the system, affected people, risks and mitigations before launch. GDPR requires a DPIA for high-risk personal-data processing.",
 "a-gov-ops-s4":"Define what counts as an AI incident, who is paged, how to contain it (for example disable a feature) and how to notify affected parties."}),

"a-cost":L(
"AI cost is a meter that runs on every token, tool call and GPU hour. A design that looks cheap in a demo can cost millions at scale because agents make many calls and output tokens cost more than input tokens. Cost modeling turns a design into dollars per task so you can compare options and set budgets before the bill arrives.",
["Count tokens per task by stage: input, cached input, output, and number of calls per task.",
 "Multiply by price per million tokens for each model, and add tool, embedding, storage, egress and eval costs.",
 "Multiply by tasks per month for low, expected and high scenarios.",
 "Apply levers: caching, routing to cheaper tiers, batch APIs, shorter outputs, fewer agent steps.",
 "Set budgets and alerts per tenant and feature, with showback or chargeback."],
"Agent task cost: 6 model calls per task, each with 3,000 input and 400 output tokens. At $3 per 1M input and $15 per 1M output: input 18,000 × $3/M = $0.054, output 2,400 × $15/M = $0.036, total $0.090 per task. At 100,000 tasks a month that's $9,000. If 2,000 of each call's input tokens are a cached prefix billed at 10%, input drops to (6×1,000×$3 + 6×2,000×$0.30)/1M = $0.018 + $0.0036 = $0.0216, total ≈ $0.058 per task, ≈ $5,760 a month.",
["Ignoring output tokens, which often cost several times more than input.","Pricing one call instead of a whole agent task with retries and tool loops.","No per-team budgets, so usage grows without anyone noticing."],
"A media company's CFO asked why AI spend tripled. A cost model per feature showed one summarization feature re-sent the full article on every follow-up question; caching the article prefix and capping follow-ups cut that feature's cost by 55%.",
["Model cost per task, not per call.","Output tokens and agent loops dominate.","Budgets and alerts belong in the design."],
"flowchart LR\n  T[Tokens per call] --> CALLS[× calls per task]\n  CALLS --> PRICE[× price per 1M]\n  PRICE --> OTHER[+ tools, embeddings, storage, evals]\n  OTHER --> VOL[× tasks per month]\n  VOL --> LEV[Levers: cache, route, batch, trim]\n  LEV --> BUD[Budgets + alerts]",
"Diagram: tokens per call times calls per task times price, plus other costs, times monthly volume, then cost levers and budgets.",
{"a-cost-units-s1":"Providers price input and output tokens separately, and output is usually 3-5× more expensive per token.",
 "a-cost-units-s2":"Prompt caching bills repeated prefixes at a steep discount, so put stable content (system prompt, tools, documents) first.",
 "a-cost-units-s3":"For self-hosting, total cost of ownership includes GPU rental or depreciation, power, networking, storage and staff time.",
 "a-cost-ctrl-s2":"Send simple requests to cheaper models and escalate only hard ones.",
 "a-cost-ctrl-s3":"Batch APIs trade latency (results within hours) for roughly half the price, good for offline enrichment."},widget="budget"),

"a-privacy":L(
"Privacy by design means the safest personal data is the data you never send. AI systems tempt teams to paste whole records into prompts, log every conversation forever and index everything. A privacy-minded architecture sends only the fields a task needs, masks the rest, limits retention, and makes sure a deletion request reaches every copy, including vector indexes.",
["Identify personal and sensitive fields in each data source.",
 "Minimize: select only the fields a task needs, and filter retrieval by the user's permissions.",
 "Redact or tokenize PII before prompts, logs and third-party tracing tools where possible.",
 "Review vendor terms: data processing agreements, training-use opt-outs, retention and region.",
 "Operate: retention schedules, deletion that reaches indexes and caches, access reviews and breach playbooks."],
"Tokenization for prompts: a support message 'Hi, I'm Ana Silva, card 4111 1111 1111 1111, my refund is missing' becomes 'Hi, I'm [NAME_1], card [CARD_1], my refund is missing' before the model call. The model's reply 'Thanks [NAME_1]…' is re-identified in the app using a mapping stored only in the support system, so the model provider and logs never see the real values.",
["Pasting production data into a consumer AI tool while debugging.","Keeping full conversation transcripts indefinitely 'for quality'.","Deleting a customer from the database but not from embeddings, caches or logs."],
"A European retailer routes customer-service prompts through a redaction service, uses an EU-region model endpoint with training use disabled, keeps transcripts for 30 days, and runs a monthly job that verifies deleted customers no longer appear in any index.",
["Send the minimum.","Redact before logs and vendors.","Deletion must reach every copy."],
"flowchart LR\n  SRC[Customer data] --> MIN[Select needed fields]\n  MIN --> RED[Redact / tokenize PII]\n  RED --> LLM[Model endpoint: region + no training]\n  LLM --> REID[Re-identify in app]\n  RED --> LOGS[(Redacted logs, short retention)]\n  DEL[Deletion request] --> ALL[DB + index + cache + logs]",
"Diagram: customer data is minimized and redacted before the model, re-identified in the app, logged in redacted form, and deletion requests reach the database, index, cache and logs.",
{"a-priv-min-s1":"Include only the fields the task needs. A shipping-status answer needs the order ID and status, not the customer's full profile.",
 "a-priv-min-s2":"Mask personal data before it reaches logs and observability vendors, which often keep data longer and are accessed by more people.",
 "a-priv-min-s5":"Check whether a provider may use your prompts for training, and turn that off by contract or setting if needed.",
 "a-priv-ops-s2":"When a record is deleted, also delete its chunks and embeddings and invalidate cached answers that used it.",
 "a-priv-ops-s5":"Some laws and contracts require processing in a region. Choose endpoints, logs and backups in that region."}),

"a-solarch":L(
"A solutions architect turns a customer's goal into a credible plan: what to build, on which platform, how much it will cost and how risky it is. Customers have heard many AI promises, so credibility comes from honest sizing, a clear reference architecture and a proof-of-value with exit criteria agreed in advance.",
["Capture functional and non-functional requirements, including security, residency and oversight.",
 "Map a vendor-neutral reference architecture (gateway, retrieval, agents, evals, guardrails, observability) onto the customer's cloud.",
 "Size cost: tokens per task by stage × tasks per month × price, plus non-model costs; compare API vs self-hosting.",
 "Design a proof of value with an eval set, success thresholds and a time box.",
 "Write the proposal: phases, customer dependencies, risks, and an exit strategy from any vendor."],
"Sizing a document-review agent: 20,000 documents a month; per document, 8,000 input tokens and 1,000 output tokens across three steps. At $3/$15 per 1M: 160M input tokens → $480, 20M output tokens → $300, total $780 a month in model cost. Add ~$400 a month for search and hosting, so ≈ $1,180 a month, compared with an estimated 1,600 analyst hours a month today.",
["Proposing a custom build when a managed platform or vendor feature would do.","Quoting only model cost and forgetting search, hosting, evals, monitoring and support.","Running a proof of concept with no agreed success criteria, so it never ends."],
"For a manufacturer, an architect proposes a six-week proof of value: 300 historical maintenance tickets as the eval set, success defined as ≥ 80% correct root-cause suggestions, built on the customer's existing Azure tenancy, with a go/no-go meeting at week six.",
["Honest sizing builds trust.","The eval set is the contract for the proof of value.","Always show the exit path."],
"flowchart LR\n  REQ[Requirements + NFRs] --> RA[Reference architecture]\n  RA --> MAP[Map to customer cloud]\n  MAP --> SIZE[Sizing + cost]\n  SIZE --> POV[Proof of value + exit criteria]\n  POV --> PROP[Proposal + roadmap]",
"Diagram: requirements lead to a reference architecture, mapped to the customer's cloud, sized and costed, validated by a proof of value, and written up as a proposal and roadmap.",
{"a-solarch-t1-s1":"Functional requirements say what the system does; non-functional ones cover latency, availability, security, residency and cost.",
 "a-solarch-t1-s3":"Mark exactly where humans approve, review or can override, because this shapes UX, staffing and compliance.",
 "a-solarch-t2-s1":"Estimate tokens for each step of the workflow (retrieve, draft, check), because multi-step agents multiply cost.",
 "a-solarch-t2-s3":"Self-hosting becomes cheaper only above a volume where GPUs stay busy. Compute that crossover explicitly.",
 "a-solarch-t3-s3":"A proof of value uses real data, a time box and a pre-agreed metric so the result is a decision, not a demo.",
 "a-solarch-t3-s5":"Show how prompts, evals and data could move to another provider so the customer isn't locked in."}),

"a-scale":L(
"At scale, an AI service must keep working when a data center fails and when one big customer floods it with traffic. Multi-tenant design keeps customers isolated and fairly served; high availability keeps the service up and its indexes consistent across regions. It is classic distributed systems with some AI-specific twists, like GPU scarcity and prefix-cache affinity.",
["Isolate tenants: separate keys, quotas, data partitions and cache namespaces.",
 "Share capacity fairly with per-tenant rate limits and weighted queues.",
 "Set recovery targets (RPO and RTO) for model artifacts, indexes and configs, and replicate accordingly.",
 "Plan traffic shifting between regions and degraded modes when capacity is short.",
 "Practice failures with game days and keep runbooks current."],
"Fair share: a GPU pool serves 3,000 requests per minute. Tenant A sends 2,400 rpm, B 500 and C 300 (3,200 total). Without fair share, everyone queues. With equal weights, each tenant gets up to 1,000 rpm; B and C are fully served (800 total), and A gets the remaining 2,200 rpm, so only A is throttled by 200 rpm.",
["Running active-active across regions with no plan for keeping indexes consistent.","One GPU pool with no per-tenant limits, so one customer can starve others.","Random load balancing that destroys prefix-cache hits."],
"A SaaS vendor puts its largest customer on dedicated capacity, smaller ones on a shared pool with weighted fair queuing, and replicates vector indexes hourly to a second region with an RPO of one hour, tested quarterly.",
["Isolation and fairness are product features.","Define RPO/RTO for indexes and models, not just databases.","Rehearse failover."],
"flowchart LR\n  TA[Tenant A] --> Q[Weighted fair queue]\n  TB[Tenant B] --> Q\n  TC[Tenant C] --> Q\n  Q --> R1[Region 1 GPU pool]\n  Q -. failover .-> R2[Region 2 GPU pool]\n  R1 <--> IDX1[(Index R1)]\n  IDX1 -- replicate --> IDX2[(Index R2)]\n  R2 <--> IDX2",
"Diagram: three tenants share a weighted fair queue that sends traffic to a primary region, with failover to a second region whose index is replicated from the first.",
{"a-scale-ten-s1":"A noisy neighbor is a tenant whose load slows everyone else. Per-tenant quotas and queues prevent it.",
 "a-scale-ten-s5":"A cache shared across tenants can return one customer's data to another. Namespace caches by tenant.",
 "a-scale-ha-s1":"RPO is how much data you can lose (for example one hour of index updates); RTO is how long recovery may take.",
 "a-scale-ha-s3":"Use DNS or global load balancers to move traffic between regions, and check that the target has capacity first.",
 "a-scale-ha-s5":"A game day deliberately breaks something in a controlled way to test runbooks and alerts."}),

"a-plat":L(
"An internal AI platform is a paved road. Instead of every team wiring up keys, evals, guardrails and dashboards on its own, the platform team builds them once and offers templates that are easy to adopt. Good platforms are treated as products with users, feedback and roadmaps, not as ticket queues.",
["Offer core services: model gateway, prompt and version registry, shared eval service, sandboxed tool runtime, cost dashboards.",
 "Package them as golden paths: a template that gives a new app tracing, evals and guardrails by default.",
 "Make self-service the default; reserve tickets for exceptions.",
 "Show cost and usage per team so chargeback is clear.",
 "Measure adoption and time to first eval, and run the platform like a product."],
"Time to first eval: before the platform, a new team needed about 3 weeks to set up keys, logging and an eval harness. With a template repo and shared eval service it takes 1 day. With 20 new AI projects a year, that saves about 20 × 14 working days ≈ 280 engineer-days.",
["Building a platform so rigid that teams bypass it with their own vendor accounts.","No product owner, so the platform becomes a backlog of tickets.","Measuring only GPU utilization instead of developer adoption and outcomes."],
"A large retailer's platform team offers a 'new AI app' template with gateway access, OpenTelemetry tracing and an eval CI job. Within a year 80% of AI projects use it, and security reviews are faster because defaults are already approved.",
["Paved roads beat mandates.","Defaults (tracing, guardrails, evals) spread good practice.","Treat the platform as a product."],
"flowchart TD\n  DEV[Product teams] --> TPL[Golden-path template]\n  TPL --> GW[Gateway]\n  TPL --> REG[Prompt registry]\n  TPL --> EVAL[Eval service]\n  TPL --> SBX[Tool sandbox]\n  TPL --> OBS[Tracing + cost dashboards]",
"Diagram: product teams start from a golden-path template that connects them to the gateway, prompt registry, eval service, tool sandbox and tracing and cost dashboards.",
{"a-plat-cap-s2":"A prompt registry stores versioned prompts with owners and eval results, so changes are reviewed like code.",
 "a-plat-cap-s4":"A sandbox runs agent tools such as code execution in isolated containers with limited network and file access.",
 "a-plat-dx-s1":"A golden path is the easiest, supported way to build something, with good defaults already wired in.",
 "a-plat-dx-s5":"Self-service lets teams start in minutes; tickets should be for exceptions like new providers or higher limits."}),

"a-cards":L(
"In regulated deals, documents are part of the system. A model card says what a model is for, how it was evaluated and where it fails. A license says what you may do with it. Procurement documents say who processes your data and how you can leave. Architects need these as much as diagrams, because missing paperwork can stop a launch.",
["Collect the model card and license for each model you use, and write a system card for your own application.",
 "Summarize evals, known limitations and intended use in plain language.",
 "Check licenses: API terms of service, open-weight licenses and any use restrictions.",
 "Run procurement checks: subprocessors, security questionnaires, data return and exit clauses.",
 "Keep a change log so documentation tracks model and prompt updates."],
"License check: a team wants to use an open-weight model in a commercial product. Its license allows commercial use but requires attribution and forbids use above a monthly-active-user threshold without a separate agreement. Legal confirms the product stays below the threshold and the UI adds the required attribution.",
["Assuming that a model hosted on Hugging Face is under a permissive license.","Writing a system card at launch and never updating it.","No plan to export prompts, evals and data when changing vendors."],
"A public-sector agency requires a system card for each AI service: purpose, data used, model versions, eval results by user group, human oversight and contact for complaints. Vendors that can't provide one are not shortlisted.",
["Cards communicate purpose, evals and limits.","Read the license before the prototype becomes a product.","Plan the exit in the contract."],
"flowchart LR\n  M[Model] --> MC[Model card + license]\n  APP[Your application] --> SC[System card]\n  MC --> SC\n  SC --> PROC[Procurement: subprocessors, security, exit]\n  PROC --> GO[Approved for use]\n  CHG[Changes] --> LOG[Change log] --> SC",
"Diagram: a model's card and license feed your application's system card, which goes through procurement before approval; a change log keeps the system card current.",
{"a-cards-doc-s1":"Model cards, introduced by Mitchell et al. in 2019, describe a model's intended use, training data, evaluation results and limitations.",
 "a-cards-doc-s2":"A system card covers the whole application: model, prompts, tools, guardrails, human oversight and evals.",
 "a-cards-buy-s1":"API terms of service govern hosted use; weight licenses govern downloaded models. Both may restrict use cases or require attribution.",
 "a-cards-buy-s4":"Contracts should say how you get your data, fine-tuned weights and logs back, and how fast the vendor deletes them."}),

"a-product":L(
"Product management for agents is about deciding what the agent should do, how much it is allowed to do on its own, and how users stay in control. The best AI PMs write specs as examples and evals, design UX that shows sources and progress, and start agents at low autonomy, raising it only when evidence shows it is safe.",
["Write the spec as examples: good outputs, bad outputs and unacceptable behaviors, then turn them into an eval set.",
 "Choose an autonomy level per action: suggest, draft, act with approval, act autonomously.",
 "Design UX for uncertainty: show sources, progress, confidence and easy undo or correction.",
 "Define metrics across outcome, quality, trust and economics.",
 "Experiment with guardrails, and raise autonomy only when metrics clear agreed thresholds."],
"Raising autonomy with evidence: an email-triage agent drafts replies that agents approve. Over 4 weeks, 2,000 drafts are approved unchanged 94% of the time, edited 5% and rejected 1%. The team agrees to auto-send only for the 'order status' category, where approval-unchanged was 99.2% over 600 drafts, and keeps approval for everything else.",
["Shipping a free-form chat box when a structured UI would be clearer and safer.","Giving the agent full autonomy on day one.","Measuring usage only, not whether tasks were completed correctly."],
"A B2B software company prices its support agent per resolved ticket instead of per seat. Because each resolution has a variable model cost, the team tracks cost per resolution weekly and caps agent steps to protect margins.",
["The eval set is the spec.","Autonomy is earned per action with evidence.","Show your work: sources, progress, undo."],
"flowchart LR\n  SPEC[Examples + unacceptable behaviors] --> EV[Eval set]\n  EV --> AUT{Autonomy level}\n  AUT --> S1[Suggest]\n  AUT --> S2[Draft]\n  AUT --> S3[Act with approval]\n  AUT --> S4[Act autonomously]\n  S3 -->|evidence| S4\n  S1 --> MET[Metrics: outcome, quality, trust, cost]",
"Diagram: example-driven specs become an eval set that informs the autonomy level, from suggest to act autonomously, with evidence moving actions up and metrics tracking results.",
{"a-product-t1-s2":"List behaviors that must never happen, such as promising refunds or sharing another customer's data, and test for them.",
 "a-product-t1-s3":"Different actions in one agent can have different autonomy, for example auto-tagging but human-approved refunds.",
 "a-product-t2-s1":"Show what the agent is doing step by step so users trust it and can stop it.",
 "a-product-t2-s3":"Make it easy to undo an action or correct an answer, and capture corrections as feedback.",
 "a-product-t3-s3":"Compare model and tool cost per task with the value of the task to make sure each use is profitable.",
 "a-product-t3-s4":"Outcome-based pricing charges per resolved task, which aligns with value but exposes you to variable costs."}),

"a-sov":L(
"Sovereign AI keeps everything inside a boundary that a government, bank or regulator controls: the model weights, the data, the logs and often the people who can access them. It is more than picking a local region. It means you can run, update, monitor and audit the system without data or control leaving the boundary.",
["Define the boundary: country, cloud, data center or air-gapped network, and who may operate inside it.",
 "Host weights, indexes, logs and embeddings inside the boundary, often on open-weight models.",
 "Use confidential computing (TEEs) where operators must not see data in use.",
 "Control the supply chain: signed model artifacts, mirrored package repositories, scanned containers.",
 "Plan operations: controlled updates, break-glass access, telemetry that doesn't exfiltrate data, and auditor evidence packs."],
"Capacity planning in-boundary: a ministry expects 2,000 users at a peak of 0.2 requests per second total per 100 users, so about 4 requests per second. With an average 800 output tokens per request, that is 3,200 output tokens per second. If one GPU node sustains 2,000 output tokens per second for the chosen model, plan for 2 nodes plus 1 spare for N+1 redundancy.",
["Selling a private endpoint to a foreign-hosted model as 'sovereign'.","No patch process for vulnerabilities in the serving stack inside an air gap.","Telemetry that sends prompts to an external vendor."],
"A defense agency runs an open-weight model on GPUs in its own facility, imports updates only as signed bundles through a controlled transfer process, and keeps all logs on site. Auditors receive an evidence pack with hashes of every deployed artifact.",
["Sovereign means operated and controlled in-boundary, not just hosted nearby.","Signed artifacts and controlled updates are essential.","Plan capacity with redundancy because you can't burst to a public cloud."],
"flowchart LR\n  subgraph B[Sovereign boundary]\n    W[Open weights] --> SRV[Serving]\n    IDX[(Local index)] --> SRV\n    SRV --> LOG[(Local logs)]\n    TEE[Confidential compute] -.-> SRV\n  end\n  EXT[External updates] -->|signed bundle| MIR[Mirror / scanner] --> W",
"Diagram: inside a sovereign boundary, open weights, a local index, serving, local logs and confidential compute; external updates enter only as signed bundles through a mirror and scanner.",
{"a-sov-ctrl-s2":"Trusted execution environments encrypt data in use and can prove (attest) what code is running, including on some modern GPUs.",
 "a-sov-ctrl-s4":"Sign model files and containers and verify signatures before deployment so tampered artifacts are rejected.",
 "a-sov-ops-s1":"In air-gapped sites, updates arrive on approved media after scanning and signature checks, so plan update cycles in advance.",
 "a-sov-ops-s3":"Monitoring must report health and metrics without sending prompts or data outside the boundary."}),

"a-org":L(
"Great architectures fail if the organization around them doesn't work. Someone has to own models, evals, incidents and vendor choices. Companies usually move from a central AI team, to a hub that sets standards while product teams build, as skills spread. The architect helps design that operating model so designs survive handover.",
["Choose an operating model: central center of excellence, federated teams, or hub-and-spoke.",
 "Split responsibilities: a platform team for shared services, product teams for use cases.",
 "Define RACI for model incidents, eval standards and vendor approval.",
 "Set pilot-to-scale criteria and track a portfolio of use cases with executive metrics.",
 "Invest in training and a skills matrix so teams can own their AI features."],
"Pilot-to-scale gate: a pilot moves to scale only if it meets (1) its eval threshold on the golden set, (2) at least 30% weekly active use among target users for four weeks, (3) cost per task within budget, and (4) security and privacy sign-off. Of 12 pilots in a year, a company scales 4 and stops 8, freeing budget early.",
["A central AI team that becomes a bottleneck for every request.","Federating to many teams with no shared eval or security standards.","Counting pilots launched instead of outcomes delivered."],
"A bank runs a hub-and-spoke model: the hub owns the gateway, eval standards and vendor list; business units build their own assistants on top. A monthly review shows each use case's adoption, quality and cost to executives.",
["Ownership is architecture too.","Hub sets standards, spokes deliver value.","Gate pilots on evidence, then scale or stop."],
"flowchart TD\n  HUB[Hub: platform, standards, vendors] --> S1[Spoke: Finance]\n  HUB --> S2[Spoke: HR]\n  HUB --> S3[Spoke: Operations]\n  S1 --> GATE{Pilot-to-scale gate}\n  S2 --> GATE\n  S3 --> GATE\n  GATE -->|pass| SCALE[Scale]\n  GATE -->|fail| STOP[Stop + learn]",
"Diagram: a hub provides platform, standards and vendors to finance, HR and operations spokes, whose pilots pass a gate to scale or stop.",
{"a-org-model-s1":"A CoE centralizes expertise; federated teams build independently; hub-and-spoke mixes central standards with distributed delivery.",
 "a-org-model-s3":"RACI says who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed for each decision or incident type.",
 "a-org-change-s1":"Agree in advance what evidence a pilot needs to scale, so decisions aren't driven by demos.",
 "a-org-change-s2":"When official tools are slow or missing, people use personal accounts. Provide a good sanctioned option quickly."}),
}
